The swbrd executables are statically linked Rust builds -- and, for
swbrd-cert-rotate, a `deno compile` bundle -- which embed a large amount of
third-party code under its own terms.  At the last audit the Rust dependency
graph was 91 third-party crates under:

    MIT OR Apache-2.0                      54
    MIT                                    20
    Apache-2.0 OR MIT                       5
    MIT/Apache-2.0                          4
    Unlicense OR MIT                        3
    Apache-2.0                              2
    BSD-2-Clause OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT       2
    (MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND Unicode-3.0     1

No copyleft in that set -- but every licence in it is an attribution licence, and
attribution licences attach to *binary* distribution: shipping the executable
means shipping the licence texts and copyright notices with it.  This package
ships none of them.

swbrd-cert-rotate is worse: it embeds the whole Deno runtime and V8, a far larger
licence set again (MIT, BSD-3-Clause and others) which has not been enumerated at
all.  OpenSSL is the one large dependency that is *not* embedded -- dtls-proxy
links it dynamically against Debian's own libssl3t64.

So: these packages are built for one private host.  They are not fit for
publication or redistribution in any form.  If you cloned this repository and are
thinking about publishing the packages it builds -- don't, until this file has
been replaced with real and complete licence information.  `cargo about generate`
or `cargo license` will produce the Rust half; the Deno half has to come out of
the Deno source tree.

Debian Policy 12.5 asks for a verbatim copy of every applicable licence right
here.  This deliberately is not that.  It is a placeholder that says so out loud,
which is safer than the wrong claim it replaces -- that the whole package is
MIT-0.

Regenerate the crate counts above with:
    cargo metadata --format-version 1 --filter-platform x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
